# Set operations — union, unionAll, intersect, except.
#
# A set combinator is a curried function (`query -> query -> query`) used as a
# pipeline step, so the left operand is whatever the pipeline has built so far
# and the right operand is passed as its argument:
#
#     left & unionAll right      -- left UNION ALL right
#
# `union` and `unionAll` differ ONLY in deduplication, exactly as in SQL:
#
#   union       UNION      — removes duplicate rows (a set union)
#   unionAll   UNION ALL  — keeps every row, duplicates included (a bag union)
#
# Because `unionAll` keeps duplicates, it is the cheaper operation: no sort or
# hash is needed to deduplicate, so prefer it whenever the duplicates are
# expected or already excluded (e.g. two date ranges of the same table).
#
# IMPORTANT — SQL matches set-operand columns POSITIONALLY, while tetaue rows
# are unordered records. The renderer therefore projects an explicit, shared
# column order on BOTH operands, which requires each operand to have a KNOWN
# schema. A bare `table "x"` with no annotation has an unknown schema and is
# rejected as a set operand — annotate it, or project it with `map` first.

# Two slices of the same shape: live users and archived users. They overlap
# on purpose (both hold Ada and Bo), so the difference between `union` and
# `unionAll` is visible on real rows. The annotations give both operands the
# same schema, in the same order.
live: query {
    id: int,
    name: string,
    active: bool,
} = table "users"

legacy: query {
    id: int,
    name: string,
    active: bool,
} = table "archived"

# --- unionAll: keep everything ------------------------------------------
# Rows from both sides together, duplicates preserved. `sort`/`take` after the
# set run on the COMBINED result, not on one operand — a set operation is a
# complete relational expression, so the pipeline continues from its output.
everyone = live
    & unionAll legacy
    & sort (u => [asc u.id, asc u.name])
    & take 25

# --- union: deduplicate ---------------------------------------------------
# Same operands, but the duplicate rows are collapsed. `users` and `archived`
# share Ada (id 1) and Bo (id 3) with the same name/active, so `unionAll`
# returns each of those twice while `union` returns them once.
distinct_everyone = live
    & union legacy
    & sort (u => [asc u.id, asc u.name])

# --- distinct after a set -------------------------------------------------
# `distinct` is a separate step that deduplicates the COMBINED result, so it
# agrees with `union` on the same operands:
#
#     live & union   legacy              -- 4 rows (Ada and Bo once each)
#     live & unionAll legacy            -- 6 rows (Ada and Bo twice)
#     live & unionAll legacy & distinct -- 4 rows, the same as `union`
#
# The point is worth stating explicitly because the naive lowering
# (`SELECT DISTINCT ... FROM live UNION ALL ...`) would apply DISTINCT to
# the LEFT OPERAND ONLY and silently keep the right operand's duplicates.
# tetaue wraps the whole set expression instead, so the deduplication covers
# every combined row.
bagdedup = live
    & unionAll legacy
    & distinct
    & sort (u => [asc u.id, asc u.name])

# --- intersect and except -------------------------------------------------
# The other two set operations are spelled the same way and require the same
# conditions (known, matching schemas). Hive supports UNION and UNION ALL but
# NOT INTERSECT or EXCEPT — those are rejected with a dialect diagnostic at
# render time for the `hive` dialect.
both = live & intersect legacy
only_live = live & except legacy

# --- operands are arbitrary relational expressions ------------------------
# Either side may be a full pipeline. Here both are filtered and projected to
# the same shape, so the set operation combines their results. A named
# intermediate used as an operand renders as a `WITH` CTE and is reused rather
# than duplicated.
active_users_step = live
    & filter (u => u.active)
    & map (u => { id = u.id, name = u.name })

active_archived_step = legacy
    & filter (u => u.active)
    & map (u => { id = u.id, name = u.name })

active_everyone = active_users_step
    & unionAll active_archived_step
    & sort (u => [asc u.id, asc u.name])

# --- one module runs ONE query --------------------------------------------
# The bindings above are library-style (exported or not, they are all reachable
# by name). Only `main` is the module's query, so the others are rendered by
# asking for them explicitly:
#
#   tetaue render examples/set-operations.tetaue --binding distinct_everyone
#   tetaue render examples/set-operations.tetaue --binding only_live --dialect postgresql
main = everyone
